---
layout: article
title: Send events over REST
description: Send Appwrite Analytics events with a plain HTTP request from any platform, such as games, desktop apps, and command-line tools.
---

Any client that can make an HTTP request can send analytics events. Use REST on platforms without an Appwrite `Tracking` class, such as a game engine, a smart TV app, a desktop app, or a command-line tool. Android, Apple, Flutter, and React Native apps have a `Tracking` class instead. See [Track a mobile app](/docs/products/analytics/mobile).

Each event is one `POST` request to your property. Client-side events need no API key.

# Send an event {% #send-an-event %}

Send the event name and the URL it happened on.

```bash
curl -X POST 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/events' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -d '{
        "name": "pageview",
        "url": "https://example.com/pricing",
        "referrer": "https://www.google.com/"
    }'
```

Appwrite responds with `204 No Content`.

An event named `pageview` or `screen_view` counts as a pageview. Any other name is a custom event. Add `props` to attach details, as a flat list that alternates keys and values.

```bash
curl -X POST 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/events' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -d '{
        "name": "signup_completed",
        "url": "https://example.com/signup",
        "props": ["plan", "pro", "source", "pricing_page"]
    }'
```

# Parameters {% #parameters %}

| Parameter | Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `name` | string | Required. The event name, up to 64 characters. |
| `url` | string | Required. The full URL the event happened on, including the scheme, such as `https://example.com/pricing`. For a native app, use your own scheme, such as `app://ios/settings`. |
| `domain` | string | The hostname Appwrite records and uses as part of the visitor ID. Defaults to the hostname of `url`. |
| `referrer` | string | The URL of the page that sent the visitor. |
| `screenWidth` | integer | The width of the window or viewport in CSS or logical pixels. |
| `scrollDepth` | integer | How far the visitor scrolled, from `0` to `100` percent. |
| `engagementTime` | integer | Seconds the visitor was active since the previous event that carried this value. |
| `sessionHash` | string | An ID for the visit, up to 128 characters. Events from the same visitor with different values count as separate visits. It doesn't change the visitor ID. |
| `props` | string[] | Custom props as alternating keys and values. Up to 32 items, so 16 key and value pairs, each up to 255 characters. |
| `interactive` | boolean | Whether the event is an interaction that ends a [bounce](/docs/products/analytics/visitors#visit-metrics). Defaults to `true` for custom events. Set `false` for background events, such as heartbeats or syncs. Views ignore it. |
| `userId`, `ip`, `userAgent` | string | Server-side only. They need an API key. See [Track events from your server](/docs/products/analytics/server-side). To record the user ID of a signed-in session from a client, turn on [Identify signed-in users](/docs/products/analytics/properties#user-identification) instead. |

Appwrite records the path of `url` without its query string and fragment. It reads UTM parameters and ad click IDs from the query string first, so `https://example.com/?utm_source=newsletter` is recorded as the page `/` with `newsletter` as its UTM source. See [Traffic sources](/docs/products/analytics/sources).

A URL without a path, such as `https://example.com` or `app://android`, is recorded as the page `/`.

# Who an event belongs to {% #visitors %}

Appwrite counts visitors from the request itself: the IP address it came from, its `User-Agent` header, and the hostname. When the event has a user ID, the user ID takes the place of the IP address and user agent. Send client events from the device, so Appwrite receives that device's IP address and user agent, and locates each user by their own address.

Appwrite reads the browser, operating system, and device from the `User-Agent` header. When it doesn't recognize the value, those fields stay empty and the event still counts. For how visitors and sessions are counted, see [Visitors and sessions](/docs/products/analytics/visitors).

# Origins {% #origins %}

Browsers add an `Origin` header to these requests, and so do the Appwrite Android, Apple, Flutter, and React Native SDKs. When a request has an `Origin` header, Appwrite checks it against the apps under **Apps** in your project. A browser origin must match a Web app's hostname. An app origin, such as `appwrite-android://com.example.shop`, needs an app for that platform under **Apps**. To accept only your own package, add its origin to the property's [allowed origins](/docs/products/analytics/properties#allowed-origins).

Native HTTP clients usually send no `Origin` header. Appwrite accepts those requests while the property allows every origin with `*`, the default. When a property's allowed origins are limited, requests without a matching `Origin` header are rejected unless they use an API key with the `analytics.write` scope.

# Errors {% #errors %}

| Status | Type | Cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `400` | `general_argument_invalid` | A parameter is missing or invalid, such as a URL without a scheme or a name over 64 characters. |
| `401` | `general_access_forbidden` | The origin isn't in the property's allowed origins, or the request sets `userId`, `ip`, or `userAgent` without an API key. |
| `402` | `limit_analytics_events_exceeded` | The organization reached its plan's monthly events limit. See [Limits and retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits#at-the-limit). |
| `403` | `general_unknown_origin` | The `Origin` header doesn't belong to an app in the project. |
| `404` | `analytics_property_not_found` | The property ID is wrong, or **Tracking** is off in the property's settings. |
